── The two entries a peering is ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

pkg/apis/kubeovn/v1/vpc.go, verbatim:

    type VpcPeering struct {
        RemoteVpc      string `json:"remoteVpc,omitempty"`
        LocalConnectIP string `json:"localConnectIP,omitempty"`
    }

    type StaticRoute struct {
        Policy     RoutePolicy `json:"policy,omitempty"`
        CIDR       string      `json:"cidr"`
        NextHopIP  string      `json:"nextHopIP"`
        ECMPMode   string      `json:"ecmpMode"`
        BfdID      string      `json:"bfdId"`
        RouteTable string      `json:"routeTable"`
    }

    const (
        PolicySrc RoutePolicy = "policySrc"
        PolicyDst RoutePolicy = "policyDst"
    )

    // in VpcSpec
    StaticRoutes []*StaticRoute `json:"staticRoutes,omitempty"`
    VpcPeerings  []*VpcPeering  `json:"vpcPeerings,omitempty"`

    // in VpcStatus
    VpcPeerings []string `json:"vpcPeerings"`

⚠ NEITHER ARRAY CARRIES AN x-kubernetes-list-type MARKER, so both are ATOMIC under server-side apply
and the last applier owns the whole list — the finding charts/managed/kube-ovn-vpc/SOURCE records
under "every array here is ATOMIC", and the reason a peering is a FRAGMENT under the network's
co-writer manager rather than an object of its own (docs/plan/09 § A second writer on an object).

── What the controller does with them ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

pkg/controller/vpc.go, handleAddOrUpdateVpc:

    for _, peering := range vpc.Spec.VpcPeerings {
        if err = util.CheckCidrs(peering.LocalConnectIP); err != nil { ... return err }
        newPeers = append(newPeers, peering.RemoteVpc)
        if err := c.OVNNbClient.CreatePeerRouterPort(vpc.Name, peering.RemoteVpc, peering.LocalConnectIP); err != nil { ... }
    }
    for _, oldPeer := range vpc.Status.VpcPeerings {
        if !slices.Contains(newPeers, oldPeer) {
            if err = c.OVNNbClient.DeleteLogicalRouterPort(fmt.Sprintf("%s-%s", vpc.Name, oldPeer)); err != nil { ... }
        }
    }
    vpc.Status.VpcPeerings = newPeers

So `status.vpcPeerings` is the list of remote names the controller has built a port for — which is
what `POST …/showRoutes` reports as `localConnected`/`remoteConnected` — and an entry REMOVED from the
spec has its port deleted on the next pass, which is what a peering's withdrawal relies on.

pkg/ovs/ovn-nb-logical_router_port.go:

    func (c *OVNNbClient) CreatePeerRouterPort(localRouter, remoteRouter, localRouterPortIP string) error

The local port is named `{localRouter}-{remoteRouter}`, its peer `{remoteRouter}-{localRouter}`, and
its `networks` are `strings.Split(localRouterPortIP, ",")` — so `localConnectIP` is `address/prefix`,
and a comma-separated pair is a dual-stack port. This type sends one v4 address per side; the v6 half
is conformance.yaml § owed, `peering-is-ipv4-only`.

⚠ formatVpc fills an empty StaticRoute.Policy with PolicyDst and issues a full Update, which makes the
controller a manager of the list the co-writers also manage. The fragment therefore carries
`policy: policyDst` explicitly, so the two agree — reasoned here, unproven on a live controller:
conformance.yaml § owed, `routing-is-unproven-until-the-vm-lane`.

⚠ Static routes are validated for shape only — `net.ParseCIDR(item.CIDR)` and `net.ParseIP(item.NextHopIP)`
— and never for reachability. A route whose next hop is the wrong end of the link is accepted and
routes nothing; NetworkPeeringTests asserts the arithmetic that keeps the two ends straight.

── Scope ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Vpc is `+kubebuilder:resource:scope="Cluster",shortName="vpc",path="vpcs"`, so both objects a peering
writes onto are cluster-scoped and the subscription and group separation is in their NAMES —
VirtualNetworks.ObjectNameOf — which is why the remote's name is qualified with THIS resource's
namespace and a body cannot reach a network outside its resource group.
